    user_requests: "User prompt: # MASTER PROMPT FOR GOOGLE AI STUDIO\n\nYou are a senior product designer, UX engineer, 3D web developer, and full-stack software architect.\n\nBuild a **complete, production-ready, responsive web application** with exceptional UI/UX design and a seamlessly integrated **interactive 3D model as the visual centerpiece**.\n\nThe application must feel like a premium, modern product\342\200\224not a generic AI-generated dashboard or template. Prioritize visual hierarchy, usability, performance, accessibility, maintainable architecture, and a polished 3D experience.\n\nDo not create only a mockup. Implement the actual frontend, interactive 3D viewer, responsive behavior, application state, and functional user flows.\n\n---\n\n# 1. PRODUCT DIRECTION\n\n## Application concept\n\nBuild a **3D-centered cybersecurity intelligence and continuous risk quantification platform**.\n\nThe application should communicate this core idea:\n\n> **Understand your cyber environment. Detect abnormal behavior. Quantify risk. Take action.**\n\nThe 3D model should represent the organization\'s **digital infrastructure / cyber-risk environment**, not merely sit beside the content as decoration.\n\n### Suggested product name\n\n**Aegis 3D \342\200\224 Continuous Cyber Risk Intelligence**\n\nYou may use a more refined name if it improves the brand identity.\n\n### Primary objective\n\nCreate a visually compelling interface where users can:\n\n* Understand their current cyber-risk posture\n* Explore a 3D representation of their infrastructure\n* Identify high-risk assets\n* View suspicious activity\n* Inspect risk propagation\n* Understand financial exposure\n* Review AI-generated recommendations\n* Navigate to detailed investigations\n\n---\n\n# 2. DESIGN PHILOSOPHY\n\nThe design must be:\n\n* Premium\n* Modern\n* Intelligent\n* Minimal but information-rich\n* Dark, cinematic, and technically sophisticated\n* Highly readable\n* Responsive\n* Accessible\n* Performance-conscious\n\nAvoid:\n\n* Generic admin-dashboard templates\n* Excessive neon effects\n* Unnecessary gradients everywhere\n* Overloaded cards\n* Random decorative animations\n* Poor contrast\n* Tiny unreadable text\n* Excessive glassmorphism\n* 3D elements that distract from the actual content\n\nThe 3D model is the **hero experience**, but the interface must remain practical and usable.\n\n---\n\n# 3. APPLICATION STRUCTURE\n\nCreate the following main pages:\n\n```text\n/\n  Landing / Overview\n\n/dashboard\n  Enterprise risk overview\n\n/infrastructure\n  Interactive 3D infrastructure explorer\n\n/assets\n  Asset inventory and risk details\n\n/detections\n  Behavioral anomalies and compromise signals\n\n/investigations\n  Investigation timeline and evidence\n\n/risk-quantification\n  Numerical risk and financial exposure\n\n/threat-intelligence\n  Threat intelligence and contextual analysis\n\n/recommendations\n  Prioritized defensive actions\n\n/reports\n  Executive and technical reports\n\n/settings\n  Application preferences and configuration\n```\n\nThe application should have a consistent shell:\n\n```text\n\342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n\342\224\202 Logo       Overview  Infrastructure  Risk  Reports   User\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\254\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\244\n\342\224\202               \342\224\202                                          \342\224\202\n\342\224\202 Sidebar       \342\224\202              Main Content                \342\224\202\n\342\224\202 Navigation    \342\224\202                                          \342\224\202\n\342\224\202               \342\224\202                                          \342\224\202\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\264\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230\n```\n\nOn mobile, convert the sidebar into a compact navigation drawer or bottom navigation where appropriate.\n\n---\n\n# 4. HERO / 3D CENTERPIECE\n\nThe primary page must feature a **large interactive 3D model viewer** occupying approximately **50\342\200\22365% of the hero visual area on desktop**, while preserving enough space for key information and actions.\n\n### Layout concept\n\n```text\n\342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n\342\224\202 Navigation                                                  \342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\254\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\244\n\342\224\202                              \342\224\202                              \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  CONTINUOUS CYBER RISK       \342\224\202                              \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  INTELLIGENCE                \342\224\202       INTERACTIVE 3D         \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                              \342\224\202       INFRASTRUCTURE         \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  Understand your risk.       \342\224\202       MODEL                  \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  Detect threats.             \342\224\202                              \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  Take action.                \342\224\202                              \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                              \342\224\202                              \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  [Explore Infrastructure]    \342\224\202                              \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  [View Risk Exposure]        \342\224\202                              \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                              \342\224\202                              \342\224\202\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\264\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230\n```\n\nThe model should be visually dominant without causing the page to feel crowded.\n\n---\n\n# 5. 3D MODEL SPECIFICATION\n\nCreate or integrate a realistic, optimized **3D cyber-infrastructure model**.\n\n## Preferred visual concept\n\nA stylized but realistic **digital infrastructure core** containing:\n\n* Central secure data center / server core\n* Network nodes\n* Server racks or server clusters\n* Router / firewall gateway\n* Cloud infrastructure elements\n* Connected endpoints\n* Subtle data-flow paths\n* Risk-highlighted assets\n* Floating analytical markers\n\nThe model should communicate:\n\n**Assets \342\206\222 Connections \342\206\222 Threat Signals \342\206\222 Risk**\n\nDo not make it look like a toy, game scene, or overly complex sci-fi spaceship.\n\n### Alternative\n\nIf a custom 3D asset is unavailable, create a procedural Three.js scene using optimized geometry and materials that visually represents:\n\n* A central infrastructure hub\n* Connected network nodes\n* Data pathways\n* Risk indicators\n* Asset clusters\n\nThe result must still look intentional and professionally designed.\n\n---\n\n# 6. 3D ASSET TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS\n\nUse **Three.js with React Three Fiber** if the project uses React.\n\nRecommended stack:\n\n* Three.js\n* React Three Fiber\n* @react-three/drei\n* GLTF / GLB assets\n* WebGL 2 where available\n\n## Asset format\n\nPrefer:\n\n```text\n.glb\n```\n\nUse compressed assets where practical.\n\n## Materials\n\nUse **PBR materials** with:\n\n* Physically based shading\n* Appropriate roughness\n* Metallic values only where physically justified\n* Normal maps where useful\n* Ambient occlusion where useful\n* Emissive materials for subtle status indicators\n* Realistic surface response\n\nAvoid making every object metallic or emissive.\n\n## Polygon budget\n\nTarget:\n\n* **Hero model:** approximately 50,000\342\200\223150,000 triangles\n* **Low-end fallback model:** approximately 10,000\342\200\22330,000 triangles\n* Individual decorative objects should be significantly simpler\n\nUse instancing for repeated objects such as:\n\n* Server units\n* Network nodes\n* Small indicators\n* Repeated architectural elements\n\n## Textures\n\nPrefer:\n\n* 1K\342\200\2232K textures for major assets\n* Compressed texture formats where supported\n* Texture atlases where beneficial\n* Avoid unnecessarily large textures\n\n## Lighting\n\nImplement realistic, performance-conscious lighting:\n\n* Soft key light\n* Subtle fill light\n* Ambient/environment lighting\n* Gentle rim lighting\n* Contact shadows where practical\n\nDo not use excessive dynamic lights.\n\nThe model should remain attractive in both dark and light accessibility modes if the application supports both.\n\n---\n\n# 7. 3D INTERACTION REQUIREMENTS\n\nThe viewer must support:\n\n### Desktop\n\n* Rotate using mouse drag\n* Zoom using scroll\n* Pan using appropriate mouse controls\n* Reset camera\n* Optional auto-rotation toggle\n\n### Mobile\n\n* One-finger rotation\n* Pinch-to-zoom\n* Two-finger pan where supported\n* Touch-friendly controls\n* No accidental page scrolling while interacting with the model\n\n### UI controls\n\nProvide accessible controls for:\n\n* Reset view\n* Rotate / pause\n* Zoom in\n* Zoom out\n* Toggle labels\n* Toggle risk overlay\n* Toggle data-flow animation\n* Fullscreen viewer where appropriate\n\nControls must be visually subtle and not cover important model content.\n\n---\n\n# 8. 3D RISK VISUALIZATION\n\nThe 3D model must connect to application data.\n\nEach major asset/node should support:\n\n* Asset name\n* Asset type\n* Risk score\n* Compromise probability\n* Business criticality\n* Current status\n* Last updated time\n\n### Visual states\n\n```text\nNormal asset       \342\206\222 neutral appearance\nLow risk           \342\206\222 subtle cool indicator\nMedium risk        \342\206\222 amber indicator\nHigh risk          \342\206\222 red/orange indicator\nCritical risk      \342\206\222 prominent but controlled alert state\nSelected asset     \342\206\222 highlighted outline / focus\n```\n\nDo not rely on color alone. Also use:\n\n* Icons\n* Labels\n* Patterns\n* Textual status\n* Accessible descriptions\n\n### Interaction flow\n\n```text\nUser selects asset\n       \342\206\223\nCamera smoothly focuses on asset\n       \342\206\223\nAsset information panel opens\n       \342\206\223\nRisk factors displayed\n       \342\206\223\nUser can inspect details\n       \342\206\223\nUser can navigate to investigation or recommendation\n```\n\n---\n\n# 9. DATA FLOW ANIMATION\n\nAdd subtle animated data-flow paths between infrastructure nodes.\n\nRequirements:\n\n* Smooth animation\n* Low CPU/GPU usage\n* No excessive flashing\n* Pause when reduced-motion is enabled\n* Pause when the viewer is not visible\n* Use animation to communicate relationships, not decoration\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nInternet\n   \342\206\223\nFirewall\n   \342\206\223\nApplication Server\n   \342\206\223\nDatabase\n```\n\nIf a simulated suspicious path is active, highlight it with a clear status indicator and explanatory label.\n\nDo not imply that the visualization represents real network traffic unless real data is connected.\n\n---\n\n# 10. PROGRESSIVE LOADING\n\nImplement progressive loading for the 3D experience.\n\n### Loading sequence\n\n```text\nPage shell loads\n      \342\206\223\nHero typography appears\n      \342\206\223\n3D placeholder / skeleton appears\n      \342\206\223\nLow-resolution model loads\n      \342\206\223\nHigh-quality model loads\n      \342\206\223\nTextures and enhancements load\n      \342\206\223\nViewer becomes fully interactive\n```\n\nUse:\n\n* Lazy loading\n* Suspense boundaries\n* GLB loading\n* Loading progress indicator\n* Asset preloading only when useful\n* Error boundaries\n\nDo not block the entire application while the 3D model loads.\n\n---\n\n# 11. LOW-END DEVICE FALLBACK\n\nDetect device and rendering capability.\n\nProvide a quality selector:\n\n* Auto\n* High\n* Medium\n* Low\n\nFor low-end devices:\n\n* Reduce pixel ratio\n* Disable expensive post-processing\n* Reduce shadow quality\n* Reduce particle count\n* Use lower-poly model\n* Reduce animation complexity\n* Disable unnecessary effects\n\nIf WebGL is unavailable:\n\nDisplay a polished fallback:\n\n```text\nInteractive 3D visualization is unavailable in this browser.\n\n[View 2D Infrastructure Map]\n[Continue to Dashboard]\n```\n\nThe fallback must preserve the same information and user flows.\n\nDo not show a blank canvas.\n\n---\n\n# 12. WEBGL IMPLEMENTATION\n\nUse Three.js or React Three Fiber.\n\nImplement:\n\n* WebGL capability detection\n* Renderer initialization\n* Responsive canvas sizing\n* Device pixel ratio limits\n* Camera configuration\n* OrbitControls or equivalent\n* Proper disposal of geometries, materials, textures\n* Animation-frame cleanup\n* Resize handling\n* Visibility-based rendering optimization\n* Error boundaries\n* Resource loading states\n\nUse `requestAnimationFrame` responsibly.\n\nAvoid unnecessary React re-renders during continuous 3D animation.\n\nKeep frequently changing 3D state inside the rendering layer where appropriate.\n\n---\n\n# 13. PERFORMANCE REQUIREMENTS\n\nTarget a smooth experience:\n\n* 60 FPS on capable desktop devices\n* Stable interaction on modern mobile devices\n* Avoid unnecessary GPU effects\n* Avoid excessive transparent objects\n* Avoid large uncompressed textures\n* Avoid unnecessary shadow maps\n* Avoid rendering when the viewer is off-screen\n* Use instancing for repeated geometry\n* Use compressed assets where practical\n\nImplement performance monitoring in development mode.\n\nDisplay optional developer metrics:\n\n* FPS\n* Draw calls\n* Triangle count\n* Texture memory estimate\n\nDo not expose technical metrics prominently to normal users.\n\n---\n\n# 14. DESIGN SYSTEM\n\nCreate a cohesive design system.\n\n## Color palette\n\nUse a dark, premium cybersecurity palette:\n\n* Deep charcoal / near-black background\n* Dark slate surfaces\n* Cool neutral text\n* Controlled cyan or blue accent\n* Amber for warnings\n* Red for critical risk\n* Green for healthy status\n\nAvoid excessive neon.\n\nEnsure all text and interactive elements meet **WCAG 2.1 AA contrast requirements**.\n\n## Typography\n\nUse a modern sans-serif typeface.\n\nSuggested hierarchy:\n\n```text\nDisplay: 48\342\200\22372px desktop\nH1: 36\342\200\22348px\nH2: 28\342\200\22336px\nH3: 20\342\200\22324px\nBody: 16px\nSmall: 13\342\200\22314px\n```\n\nUse responsive scaling.\n\n## Spacing\n\nUse a consistent spacing scale:\n\n```text\n4 / 8 / 12 / 16 / 24 / 32 / 48 / 64\n```\n\n## Border radius\n\nUse a consistent radius system.\n\nAvoid excessive rounded cards that make the interface look generic.\n\n---\n\n# 15. MICRO-INTERACTIONS\n\nImplement subtle, purposeful interactions:\n\n* Button hover transitions\n* Card elevation changes\n* Smooth panel transitions\n* Asset selection feedback\n* Risk-score count-up animation\n* Loading shimmer\n* Toast notifications\n* Smooth camera focus\n* Tooltip appearance\n* Tab transitions\n\nRespect:\n\n```css\nprefers-reduced-motion: reduce\n```\n\nWhen reduced motion is enabled:\n\n* Disable auto-rotation\n* Reduce transitions\n* Stop animated data flows\n* Avoid parallax\n* Avoid flashing effects\n\n---\n\n# 16. NAVIGATION AND USER FLOWS\n\nDesign clear primary flows.\n\n## Flow A \342\200\224 Understand enterprise risk\n\n```text\nDashboard\n   \342\206\223\nView overall risk\n   \342\206\223\nExplore top risk assets\n   \342\206\223\nOpen 3D infrastructure\n   \342\206\223\nSelect high-risk asset\n   \342\206\223\nView risk explanation\n```\n\n## Flow B \342\200\224 Investigate suspicious behavior\n\n```text\nDetections\n   \342\206\223\nSelect anomaly\n   \342\206\223\nView evidence\n   \342\206\223\nInspect affected asset in 3D\n   \342\206\223\nReview compromise probability\n   \342\206\223\nOpen investigation\n```\n\n## Flow C \342\200\224 Prioritize remediation\n\n```text\nRisk Quantification\n   \342\206\223\nView highest expected loss\n   \342\206\223\nOpen asset\n   \342\206\223\nView recommended action\n   \342\206\223\nReview estimated risk reduction\n   \342\206\223\nCreate remediation task\n```\n\n---\n\n# 17. CALLS TO ACTION\n\nUse clear, action-oriented CTAs.\n\nPrimary:\n\n* **Explore Infrastructure**\n* **View Risk Exposure**\n* **Investigate Anomaly**\n* **Review Recommendations**\n\nSecondary:\n\n* View details\n* Compare risk\n* Open report\n* Reset view\n* Learn more\n\nAvoid vague CTAs such as:\n\n* Click here\n* Explore now\n* Get started\n\nunless the context makes their meaning clear.\n\n---\n\n# 18. ACCESSIBILITY \342\200\224 WCAG 2.1 AA\n\nImplement accessibility throughout the application.\n\nRequirements:\n\n* Semantic HTML\n* Keyboard navigation\n* Visible focus indicators\n* Proper heading hierarchy\n* Accessible labels\n* Accessible form controls\n* Sufficient color contrast\n* Screen-reader-friendly status messages\n* No color-only information\n* Skip navigation link\n* Accessible modal dialogs\n* Accessible tooltips\n* Reduced-motion support\n* Touch targets approximately 44\303\22744 CSS pixels where practical\n\n### 3D accessibility\n\nThe 3D viewer must have:\n\n* A descriptive accessible label\n* Keyboard controls\n* Text alternative / 2D infrastructure map\n* Asset information available outside the canvas\n* Accessible selected-asset details\n* Non-visual equivalent of risk information\n\nDo not make the 3D canvas the only way to access important information.\n\n---\n\n# 19. RESPONSIVE DESIGN\n\nSupport:\n\n* Desktop\n* Laptop\n* Tablet\n* Mobile\n\n### Desktop\n\nLarge 3D hero and multi-column layouts.\n\n### Tablet\n\nBalanced 3D viewer and content sections.\n\n### Mobile\n\n* 3D viewer becomes a prominent but appropriately sized card\n* Controls remain touch-friendly\n* Content stacks vertically\n* Navigation becomes compact\n* Tables become scrollable or card-based\n* No horizontal overflow\n* Avoid excessive model detail that harms performance\n\nTest at common viewport sizes.\n\n---\n\n# 20. FRONTEND ARCHITECTURE\n\nUse a maintainable component structure.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nsrc/\n  components/\n    layout/\n    navigation/\n    ui/\n    dashboard/\n    risk/\n    infrastructure/\n    three/\n  pages/\n  hooks/\n  services/\n  stores/\n  types/\n  utils/\n  styles/\n```\n\nCreate reusable components:\n\n* `AppShell`\n* `Sidebar`\n* `TopNavigation`\n* `RiskScoreCard`\n* `AssetRiskCard`\n* `InfrastructureViewer`\n* `AssetDetailPanel`\n* `RiskLegend`\n* `ViewerControls`\n* `LoadingState`\n* `ErrorState`\n* `WebGLFallback`\n* `AccessibleDataTable`\n\nDo not duplicate layout or styling logic unnecessarily.\n\n---\n\n# 21. STATE MANAGEMENT\n\nUse a suitable state-management approach.\n\nSeparate:\n\n* UI state\n* Viewer state\n* Application data\n* Loading state\n* Error state\n* User preferences\n\nPersist only appropriate preferences, such as:\n\n* Theme\n* Viewer quality\n* Reduced-motion preference\n* Sidebar state\n\nDo not store secrets in local storage.\n\n---\n\n# 22. BACKEND INTEGRATION\n\nIf backend functionality is included, use a clean API layer.\n\nSupport endpoints such as:\n\n```text\nGET /api/dashboard/summary\nGET /api/assets\nGET /api/assets/{id}\nGET /api/assets/{id}/risk\nGET /api/detections\nGET /api/risk/trends\nGET /api/recommendations\nGET /api/infrastructure/graph\n```\n\nUse typed API responses.\n\nThe 3D viewer should consume infrastructure data from the API rather than hard-coding all asset information into the component.\n\nFor initial development, provide realistic synthetic seed data.\n\nClearly label demo data.\n\n---\n\n# 23. REAL-TIME UPDATES\n\nSupport real-time or near-real-time updates using:\n\n* WebSockets\n* Server-Sent Events\n* Polling fallback\n\nWhen risk changes:\n\n* Update risk cards\n* Update affected asset state\n* Update 3D indicator\n* Update risk trend\n* Show a subtle notification\n* Avoid disruptive page reloads\n\n---\n\n# 24. ERROR HANDLING\n\nCreate polished states for:\n\n* 3D model loading\n* 3D model failure\n* WebGL unavailable\n* API unavailable\n* Empty data\n* Network timeout\n* Unauthorized access\n* Invalid asset\n* Failed report generation\n\nEvery error state must provide a useful next action.\n\n---\n\n# 25. SECURITY REQUIREMENTS\n\nImplement secure frontend practices:\n\n* Validate API responses\n* Avoid unsafe HTML injection\n* Sanitize user-generated content\n* Use secure authentication integration\n* Avoid exposing secrets\n* Configure strict CORS on the backend\n* Use HTTPS in production\n* Avoid embedding untrusted scripts\n* Apply Content Security Policy where practical\n* Do not execute arbitrary code from user input\n\nIf file uploads or external 3D assets are supported:\n\n* Validate file type\n* Validate file size\n* Restrict upload sources\n* Sanitize filenames\n* Do not execute uploaded files\n* Load only trusted model resources\n\n---\n\n# 26. TESTING\n\nImplement:\n\n### Unit tests\n\n* UI components\n* Risk formatting\n* Data transformation\n* Viewer state\n* Accessibility helpers\n\n### Integration tests\n\n* API integration\n* Asset selection\n* Risk updates\n* Navigation\n* Error states\n\n### End-to-end tests\n\nUse Playwright or equivalent.\n\nTest:\n\n```text\nOpen dashboard\n\342\206\222 Navigate to infrastructure\n\342\206\222 Load 3D viewer\n\342\206\222 Select asset\n\342\206\222 Open risk details\n\342\206\222 Navigate to recommendations\n```\n\n### Accessibility testing\n\nUse:\n\n* axe-core\n* Keyboard navigation testing\n* Contrast testing\n\n### Performance testing\n\nMeasure:\n\n* Initial load\n* 3D asset load time\n* Interaction responsiveness\n* Mobile performance\n* WebGL fallback behavior\n\n---\n\n# 27. CODE QUALITY\n\nUse:\n\n* TypeScript strict mode\n* ESLint\n* Prettier\n* Clear naming\n* Small reusable components\n* No unnecessary `any`\n* No dead code\n* No duplicated constants\n* No hard-coded secrets\n* No fake functionality presented as complete\n\nDocument complex 3D rendering decisions.\n\n---\n\n# 28. CI/CD\n\nCreate a CI pipeline that runs:\n\n```text\nInstall dependencies\n      \342\206\223\nLint\n      \342\206\223\nType check\n      \342\206\223\nUnit tests\n      \342\206\223\nBuild\n      \342\206\223\nAccessibility checks\n      \342\206\223\nEnd-to-end tests\n      \342\206\223\nProduction artifact\n```\n\nAdd dependency and security scanning.\n\n---\n\n# 29. DEPLOYMENT\n\nProvide deployment configuration for:\n\n* Local development\n* Vercel / Netlify / equivalent frontend hosting\n* Cloud deployment\n* Optional Docker deployment\n\nEnsure:\n\n* Environment variables are documented\n* Production build works\n* Asset paths work in production\n* 3D model loading works from the deployed origin\n* CORS is configured correctly\n* Error handling works without exposing stack traces\n\n---\n\n# 30. DOCUMENTATION\n\nCreate:\n\n```text\nREADME.md\nARCHITECTURE.md\n3D_IMPLEMENTATION.md\nDESIGN_SYSTEM.md\nACCESSIBILITY.md\nPERFORMANCE.md\nDEPLOYMENT.md\nTESTING.md\nCONTRIBUTING.md\n```\n\nExplain:\n\n* How the 3D viewer works\n* How assets are loaded\n* How the model is optimized\n* How risk data connects to the model\n* How the fallback works\n* How to add new assets\n* How to run the project\n* How to deploy it\n\n---\n\n# 31. IMPLEMENTATION STRATEGY\n\nBuild in the following order:\n\n## Phase 1 \342\200\224 Foundation\n\n* Initialize project\n* Configure TypeScript\n* Set up routing\n* Create design system\n* Build application shell\n\n## Phase 2 \342\200\224 Core UI\n\n* Dashboard\n* Navigation\n* Risk cards\n* Asset panels\n* Responsive layout\n\n## Phase 3 \342\200\224 3D Viewer\n\n* Three.js / React Three Fiber setup\n* Camera\n* Lighting\n* Controls\n* Model loading\n* Responsive canvas\n\n## Phase 4 \342\200\224 3D Intelligence\n\n* Asset mapping\n* Risk indicators\n* Selection\n* Camera focus\n* Data-flow animation\n* Risk overlays\n\n## Phase 5 \342\200\224 Backend Integration\n\n* API layer\n* Synthetic data\n* Asset data\n* Risk updates\n* Real-time state\n\n## Phase 6 \342\200\224 Quality\n\n* Accessibility\n* Performance optimization\n* Error states\n* Testing\n* Deployment\n\n---\n\n# 32. FINAL ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\n\nThe application must satisfy all of the following:\n\n* [ ] Premium, cohesive UI/UX\n* [ ] Responsive across desktop, tablet, and mobile\n* [ ] Interactive 3D centerpiece\n* [ ] Realistic PBR materials\n* [ ] Appropriate lighting\n* [ ] Rotate, zoom, and pan controls\n* [ ] Touch gesture support\n* [ ] Progressive 3D loading\n* [ ] Low-end device optimization\n* [ ] WebGL fallback\n* [ ] Accessible 2D alternative\n* [ ] WCAG 2.1 AA-oriented implementation\n* [ ] Risk-linked 3D assets\n* [ ] Functional navigation\n* [ ] Realistic application data\n* [ ] Error and loading states\n* [ ] Secure API integration\n* [ ] Tests\n* [ ] CI/CD\n* [ ] Documentation\n* [ ] Production build\n\n---\n\n# 33. IMPORTANT EXECUTION RULES\n\n1. Do not generate a static mockup only.\n2. Do not use a placeholder cube as the final 3D centerpiece.\n3. Do not make the 3D viewer purely decorative.\n4. Do not sacrifice usability for visual effects.\n5. Do not use excessive animations.\n6. Do not hard-code sensitive data.\n7. Do not fabricate real cybersecurity incidents.\n8. Use synthetic data for demonstrations.\n9. Clearly distinguish simulated data from live integrations.\n10. Ensure important information remains accessible without WebGL.\n11. Implement real loading, error, and fallback states.\n12. Keep the code modular and maintainable.\n13. Test the application before declaring it complete.\n14. Provide setup and deployment instructions.\n15. If a requested feature cannot be fully implemented, explain the limitation and provide the best working alternative rather than creating a fake feature.\n\n---\n\n# 34. FINAL DELIVERABLE\n\nGenerate the complete application with:\n\n* Source code\n* Functional frontend\n* Interactive 3D viewer\n* Responsive design\n* Synthetic cybersecurity data\n* Risk visualization\n* API integration structure\n* Tests\n* CI/CD configuration\n* Documentation\n* Deployment instructions\n\nAt the end, provide:\n\n1. Project structure\n2. Installation commands\n3. Environment variables\n4. Development commands\n5. Production build commands\n6. Feature completion status\n7. Known limitations\n8. Recommended next improvements\n\nBuild the application as a **polished, production-oriented 3D cybersecurity intelligence experience**, with the 3D model serving as the central interface for understanding infrastructure and cyber risk.\n\n\nHere is the full initial file tree for the application:\n/.env.example\n/.gitignore\n/index.html\n/metadata.json\n/package.json\n/public/assets/aistudio/.gitignore\n/src/App.tsx\n/src/index.css\n/src/main.tsx\n/tsconfig.json\n/vite.config.ts\n\nThe following dependencies are installed:\n@google/genai: ^2.4.0\n@tailwindcss/vite: ^4.1.14\n@vitejs/plugin-react: ^5.0.4\ndotenv: ^17.2.3\nexpress: ^4.21.2\nlucide-react: ^0.546.0\nmotion: ^12.23.24\nreact: ^19.0.1\nreact-dom: ^19.0.1\nvite: ^6.2.3\n\nThe following devDependencies are installed:\n@types/express: ^4.17.21\n@types/node: ^22.14.0\nautoprefixer: ^10.4.21\nesbuild: ^0.25.0\ntailwindcss: ^4.1.14\ntsx: ^4.21.0\ntypescript: ~5.8.2\nvite: ^6.2.3\n\nKey file contents:\n\n--- /.env.example ---\n```\n# GEMINI_API_KEY: Required for Gemini AI API calls.\n# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime from user secrets.\n# Users configure this via the Secrets panel in the AI Studio UI.\nGEMINI_API_KEY=\"MY_GEMINI_API_KEY\"\n\n# APP_URL: The URL where this applet is hosted.\n# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime with the Cloud Run service URL.\n# Used for self-referential links, OAuth callbacks, and API endpoints.\nAPP_URL=\"MY_APP_URL\"\n\n```\n\n--- /index.html ---\n```html\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <title>My Google AI Studio App</title>\n    <meta name=\"description\" content=\"An application built with Google AI Studio.\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"My Google AI Studio App\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:description\" content=\"An application built with Google AI Studio.\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"website\" />\n    <meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\" />\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div id=\"root\"></div>\n    <script type=\"module\" src=\"/src/main.tsx\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n\n\n```\n\n--- /metadata.json ---\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"\",\n  \"description\": \"\",\n  \"requestFramePermissions\": [],\n  \"majorCapabilities\": [\"MAJOR_CAPABILITY_SERVER_SIDE_GEMINI_API\"]\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /src/App.tsx ---\n```typescript\n/**\n * @license\n * SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n */\n\nexport default function App() {\n  return <div></div>;\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /src/index.css ---\n```css\n@import \"tailwindcss\";\n\n```\n\n--- /src/main.tsx ---\n```typescript\nimport {StrictMode} from \'react\';\nimport {createRoot} from \'react-dom/client\';\nimport App from \'./App.tsx\';\nimport \'./index.css\';\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(\'root\')!).render(\n  <StrictMode>\n    <App />\n  </StrictMode>,\n);\n\n```\n\n--- /tsconfig.json ---\n```json\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"target\": \"ES2022\",\n    \"experimentalDecorators\": true,\n    \"useDefineForClassFields\": false,\n    \"module\": \"ESNext\",\n    \"lib\": [\n      \"ES2022\",\n      \"DOM\",\n      \"DOM.Iterable\"\n    ],\n    \"skipLibCheck\": true,\n    \"moduleResolution\": \"bundler\",\n    \"isolatedModules\": true,\n    \"moduleDetection\": \"force\",\n    \"allowJs\": true,\n    \"jsx\": \"react-jsx\",\n    \"paths\": {\n      \"@/*\": [\n        \"./*\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"allowImportingTsExtensions\": true,\n    \"noEmit\": true\n  }\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /vite.config.ts ---\n```typescript\nimport tailwindcss from \'@tailwindcss/vite\';\nimport react from \'@vitejs/plugin-react\';\nimport path from \'path\';\nimport {defineConfig} from \'vite\';\n\nexport default defineConfig(() => {\n  return {\n    plugins: [react(), tailwindcss()],\n    resolve: {\n      alias: {\n        \'@\': path.resolve(__dirname, \'.\'),\n      },\n    },\n    server: {\n      // HMR is disabled in AI Studio via DISABLE_HMR env var.\n      // Do not modify\303\242\302\200\302\224file watching is disabled to prevent flickering during agent edits.\n      hmr: process.env.DISABLE_HMR !== \'true\',\n      // Disable file watching when DISABLE_HMR is true to save CPU during agent edits.\n      watch: process.env.DISABLE_HMR === \'true\' ? null : {},\n    },\n  };\n});\n\n```"
    user_requests: "# MASTER PROMPT FOR GOOGLE AI STUDIO\n\nYou are a senior product designer, UX engineer, 3D web developer, and full-stack software architect.\n\nBuild a **complete, production-ready, responsive web application** with exceptional UI/UX design and a seamlessly integrated **interactive 3D model as the visual centerpiece**.\n\nThe application must feel like a premium, modern product\342\200\224not a generic AI-generated dashboard or template. Prioritize visual hierarchy, usability, performance, accessibility, maintainable architecture, and a polished 3D experience.\n\nDo not create only a mockup. Implement the actual frontend, interactive 3D viewer, responsive behavior, application state, and functional user flows.\n\n---\n\n# 1. PRODUCT DIRECTION\n\n## Application concept\n\nBuild a **3D-centered cybersecurity intelligence and continuous risk quantification platform**.\n\nThe application should communicate this core idea:\n\n> **Understand your cyber environment. Detect abnormal behavior. Quantify risk. Take action.**\n\nThe 3D model should represent the organization\'s **digital infrastructure / cyber-risk environment**, not merely sit beside the content as decoration.\n\n### Suggested product name\n\n**Aegis 3D \342\200\224 Continuous Cyber Risk Intelligence**\n\nYou may use a more refined name if it improves the brand identity.\n\n### Primary objective\n\nCreate a visually compelling interface where users can:\n\n* Understand their current cyber-risk posture\n* Explore a 3D representation of their infrastructure\n* Identify high-risk assets\n* View suspicious activity\n* Inspect risk propagation\n* Understand financial exposure\n* Review AI-generated recommendations\n* Navigate to detailed investigations\n\n---\n\n# 2. DESIGN PHILOSOPHY\n\nThe design must be:\n\n* Premium\n* Modern\n* Intelligent\n* Minimal but information-rich\n* Dark, cinematic, and technically sophisticated\n* Highly readable\n* Responsive\n* Accessible\n* Performance-conscious\n\nAvoid:\n\n* Generic admin-dashboard templates\n* Excessive neon effects\n* Unnecessary gradients everywhere\n* Overloaded cards\n* Random decorative animations\n* Poor contrast\n* Tiny unreadable text\n* Excessive glassmorphism\n* 3D elements that distract from the actual content\n\nThe 3D model is the **hero experience**, but the interface must remain practical and usable.\n\n---\n\n# 3. APPLICATION STRUCTURE\n\nCreate the following main pages:\n\n```text\n/\n  Landing / Overview\n\n/dashboard\n  Enterprise risk overview\n\n/infrastructure\n  Interactive 3D infrastructure explorer\n\n/assets\n  Asset inventory and risk details\n\n/detections\n  Behavioral anomalies and compromise signals\n\n/investigations\n  Investigation timeline and evidence\n\n/risk-quantification\n  Numerical risk and financial exposure\n\n/threat-intelligence\n  Threat intelligence and contextual analysis\n\n/recommendations\n  Prioritized defensive actions\n\n/reports\n  Executive and technical reports\n\n/settings\n  Application preferences and configuration\n```\n\nThe application should have a consistent shell:\n\n```text\n\342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n\342\224\202 Logo       Overview  Infrastructure  Risk  Reports   User\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\254\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\244\n\342\224\202               \342\224\202                                          \342\224\202\n\342\224\202 Sidebar       \342\224\202              Main Content                \342\224\202\n\342\224\202 Navigation    \342\224\202                                          \342\224\202\n\342\224\202               \342\224\202                                          \342\224\202\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\264\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230\n```\n\nOn mobile, convert the sidebar into a compact navigation drawer or bottom navigation where appropriate.\n\n---\n\n# 4. HERO / 3D CENTERPIECE\n\nThe primary page must feature a **large interactive 3D model viewer** occupying approximately **50\342\200\22365% of the hero visual area on desktop**, while preserving enough space for key information and actions.\n\n### Layout concept\n\n```text\n\342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n\342\224\202 Navigation                                                  \342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\254\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\244\n\342\224\202                              \342\224\202                              \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  CONTINUOUS CYBER RISK       \342\224\202                              \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  INTELLIGENCE                \342\224\202       INTERACTIVE 3D         \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                              \342\224\202       INFRASTRUCTURE         \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  Understand your risk.       \342\224\202       MODEL                  \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  Detect threats.             \342\224\202                              \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  Take action.                \342\224\202                              \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                              \342\224\202                              \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  [Explore Infrastructure]    \342\224\202                              \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  [View Risk Exposure]        \342\224\202                              \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                              \342\224\202                              \342\224\202\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\264\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230\n```\n\nThe model should be visually dominant without causing the page to feel crowded.\n\n---\n\n# 5. 3D MODEL SPECIFICATION\n\nCreate or integrate a realistic, optimized **3D cyber-infrastructure model**.\n\n## Preferred visual concept\n\nA stylized but realistic **digital infrastructure core** containing:\n\n* Central secure data center / server core\n* Network nodes\n* Server racks or server clusters\n* Router / firewall gateway\n* Cloud infrastructure elements\n* Connected endpoints\n* Subtle data-flow paths\n* Risk-highlighted assets\n* Floating analytical markers\n\nThe model should communicate:\n\n**Assets \342\206\222 Connections \342\206\222 Threat Signals \342\206\222 Risk**\n\nDo not make it look like a toy, game scene, or overly complex sci-fi spaceship.\n\n### Alternative\n\nIf a custom 3D asset is unavailable, create a procedural Three.js scene using optimized geometry and materials that visually represents:\n\n* A central infrastructure hub\n* Connected network nodes\n* Data pathways\n* Risk indicators\n* Asset clusters\n\nThe result must still look intentional and professionally designed.\n\n---\n\n# 6. 3D ASSET TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS\n\nUse **Three.js with React Three Fiber** if the project uses React.\n\nRecommended stack:\n\n* Three.js\n* React Three Fiber\n* @react-three/drei\n* GLTF / GLB assets\n* WebGL 2 where available\n\n## Asset format\n\nPrefer:\n\n```text\n.glb\n```\n\nUse compressed assets where practical.\n\n## Materials\n\nUse **PBR materials** with:\n\n* Physically based shading\n* Appropriate roughness\n* Metallic values only where physically justified\n* Normal maps where useful\n* Ambient occlusion where useful\n* Emissive materials for subtle status indicators\n* Realistic surface response\n\nAvoid making every object metallic or emissive.\n\n## Polygon budget\n\nTarget:\n\n* **Hero model:** approximately 50,000\342\200\223150,000 triangles\n* **Low-end fallback model:** approximately 10,000\342\200\22330,000 triangles\n* Individual decorative objects should be significantly simpler\n\nUse instancing for repeated objects such as:\n\n* Server units\n* Network nodes\n* Small indicators\n* Repeated architectural elements\n\n## Textures\n\nPrefer:\n\n* 1K\342\200\2232K textures for major assets\n* Compressed texture formats where supported\n* Texture atlases where beneficial\n* Avoid unnecessarily large textures\n\n## Lighting\n\nImplement realistic, performance-conscious lighting:\n\n* Soft key light\n* Subtle fill light\n* Ambient/environment lighting\n* Gentle rim lighting\n* Contact shadows where practical\n\nDo not use excessive dynamic lights.\n\nThe model should remain attractive in both dark and light accessibility modes if the application supports both.\n\n---\n\n# 7. 3D INTERACTION REQUIREMENTS\n\nThe viewer must support:\n\n### Desktop\n\n* Rotate using mouse drag\n* Zoom using scroll\n* Pan using appropriate mouse controls\n* Reset camera\n* Optional auto-rotation toggle\n\n### Mobile\n\n* One-finger rotation\n* Pinch-to-zoom\n* Two-finger pan where supported\n* Touch-friendly controls\n* No accidental page scrolling while interacting with the model\n\n### UI controls\n\nProvide accessible controls for:\n\n* Reset view\n* Rotate / pause\n* Zoom in\n* Zoom out\n* Toggle labels\n* Toggle risk overlay\n* Toggle data-flow animation\n* Fullscreen viewer where appropriate\n\nControls must be visually subtle and not cover important model content.\n\n---\n\n# 8. 3D RISK VISUALIZATION\n\nThe 3D model must connect to application data.\n\nEach major asset/node should support:\n\n* Asset name\n* Asset type\n* Risk score\n* Compromise probability\n* Business criticality\n* Current status\n* Last updated time\n\n### Visual states\n\n```text\nNormal asset       \342\206\222 neutral appearance\nLow risk           \342\206\222 subtle cool indicator\nMedium risk        \342\206\222 amber indicator\nHigh risk          \342\206\222 red/orange indicator\nCritical risk      \342\206\222 prominent but controlled alert state\nSelected asset     \342\206\222 highlighted outline / focus\n```\n\nDo not rely on color alone. Also use:\n\n* Icons\n* Labels\n* Patterns\n* Textual status\n* Accessible descriptions\n\n### Interaction flow\n\n```text\nUser selects asset\n       \342\206\223\nCamera smoothly focuses on asset\n       \342\206\223\nAsset information panel opens\n       \342\206\223\nRisk factors displayed\n       \342\206\223\nUser can inspect details\n       \342\206\223\nUser can navigate to investigation or recommendation\n```\n\n---\n\n# 9. DATA FLOW ANIMATION\n\nAdd subtle animated data-flow paths between infrastructure nodes.\n\nRequirements:\n\n* Smooth animation\n* Low CPU/GPU usage\n* No excessive flashing\n* Pause when reduced-motion is enabled\n* Pause when the viewer is not visible\n* Use animation to communicate relationships, not decoration\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nInternet\n   \342\206\223\nFirewall\n   \342\206\223\nApplication Server\n   \342\206\223\nDatabase\n```\n\nIf a simulated suspicious path is active, highlight it with a clear status indicator and explanatory label.\n\nDo not imply that the visualization represents real network traffic unless real data is connected.\n\n---\n\n# 10. PROGRESSIVE LOADING\n\nImplement progressive loading for the 3D experience.\n\n### Loading sequence\n\n```text\nPage shell loads\n      \342\206\223\nHero typography appears\n      \342\206\223\n3D placeholder / skeleton appears\n      \342\206\223\nLow-resolution model loads\n      \342\206\223\nHigh-quality model loads\n      \342\206\223\nTextures and enhancements load\n      \342\206\223\nViewer becomes fully interactive\n```\n\nUse:\n\n* Lazy loading\n* Suspense boundaries\n* GLB loading\n* Loading progress indicator\n* Asset preloading only when useful\n* Error boundaries\n\nDo not block the entire application while the 3D model loads.\n\n---\n\n# 11. LOW-END DEVICE FALLBACK\n\nDetect device and rendering capability.\n\nProvide a quality selector:\n\n* Auto\n* High\n* Medium\n* Low\n\nFor low-end devices:\n\n* Reduce pixel ratio\n* Disable expensive post-processing\n* Reduce shadow quality\n* Reduce particle count\n* Use lower-poly model\n* Reduce animation complexity\n* Disable unnecessary effects\n\nIf WebGL is unavailable:\n\nDisplay a polished fallback:\n\n```text\nInteractive 3D visualization is unavailable in this browser.\n\n[View 2D Infrastructure Map]\n[Continue to Dashboard]\n```\n\nThe fallback must preserve the same information and user flows.\n\nDo not show a blank canvas.\n\n---\n\n# 12. WEBGL IMPLEMENTATION\n\nUse Three.js or React Three Fiber.\n\nImplement:\n\n* WebGL capability detection\n* Renderer initialization\n* Responsive canvas sizing\n* Device pixel ratio limits\n* Camera configuration\n* OrbitControls or equivalent\n* Proper disposal of geometries, materials, textures\n* Animation-frame cleanup\n* Resize handling\n* Visibility-based rendering optimization\n* Error boundaries\n* Resource loading states\n\nUse `requestAnimationFrame` responsibly.\n\nAvoid unnecessary React re-renders during continuous 3D animation.\n\nKeep frequently changing 3D state inside the rendering layer where appropriate.\n\n---\n\n# 13. PERFORMANCE REQUIREMENTS\n\nTarget a smooth experience:\n\n* 60 FPS on capable desktop devices\n* Stable interaction on modern mobile devices\n* Avoid unnecessary GPU effects\n* Avoid excessive transparent objects\n* Avoid large uncompressed textures\n* Avoid unnecessary shadow maps\n* Avoid rendering when the viewer is off-screen\n* Use instancing for repeated geometry\n* Use compressed assets where practical\n\nImplement performance monitoring in development mode.\n\nDisplay optional developer metrics:\n\n* FPS\n* Draw calls\n* Triangle count\n* Texture memory estimate\n\nDo not expose technical metrics prominently to normal users.\n\n---\n\n# 14. DESIGN SYSTEM\n\nCreate a cohesive design system.\n\n## Color palette\n\nUse a dark, premium cybersecurity palette:\n\n* Deep charcoal / near-black background\n* Dark slate surfaces\n* Cool neutral text\n* Controlled cyan or blue accent\n* Amber for warnings\n* Red for critical risk\n* Green for healthy status\n\nAvoid excessive neon.\n\nEnsure all text and interactive elements meet **WCAG 2.1 AA contrast requirements**.\n\n## Typography\n\nUse a modern sans-serif typeface.\n\nSuggested hierarchy:\n\n```text\nDisplay: 48\342\200\22372px desktop\nH1: 36\342\200\22348px\nH2: 28\342\200\22336px\nH3: 20\342\200\22324px\nBody: 16px\nSmall: 13\342\200\22314px\n```\n\nUse responsive scaling.\n\n## Spacing\n\nUse a consistent spacing scale:\n\n```text\n4 / 8 / 12 / 16 / 24 / 32 / 48 / 64\n```\n\n## Border radius\n\nUse a consistent radius system.\n\nAvoid excessive rounded cards that make the interface look generic.\n\n---\n\n# 15. MICRO-INTERACTIONS\n\nImplement subtle, purposeful interactions:\n\n* Button hover transitions\n* Card elevation changes\n* Smooth panel transitions\n* Asset selection feedback\n* Risk-score count-up animation\n* Loading shimmer\n* Toast notifications\n* Smooth camera focus\n* Tooltip appearance\n* Tab transitions\n\nRespect:\n\n```css\nprefers-reduced-motion: reduce\n```\n\nWhen reduced motion is enabled:\n\n* Disable auto-rotation\n* Reduce transitions\n* Stop animated data flows\n* Avoid parallax\n* Avoid flashing effects\n\n---\n\n# 16. NAVIGATION AND USER FLOWS\n\nDesign clear primary flows.\n\n## Flow A \342\200\224 Understand enterprise risk\n\n```text\nDashboard\n   \342\206\223\nView overall risk\n   \342\206\223\nExplore top risk assets\n   \342\206\223\nOpen 3D infrastructure\n   \342\206\223\nSelect high-risk asset\n   \342\206\223\nView risk explanation\n```\n\n## Flow B \342\200\224 Investigate suspicious behavior\n\n```text\nDetections\n   \342\206\223\nSelect anomaly\n   \342\206\223\nView evidence\n   \342\206\223\nInspect affected asset in 3D\n   \342\206\223\nReview compromise probability\n   \342\206\223\nOpen investigation\n```\n\n## Flow C \342\200\224 Prioritize remediation\n\n```text\nRisk Quantification\n   \342\206\223\nView highest expected loss\n   \342\206\223\nOpen asset\n   \342\206\223\nView recommended action\n   \342\206\223\nReview estimated risk reduction\n   \342\206\223\nCreate remediation task\n```\n\n---\n\n# 17. CALLS TO ACTION\n\nUse clear, action-oriented CTAs.\n\nPrimary:\n\n* **Explore Infrastructure**\n* **View Risk Exposure**\n* **Investigate Anomaly**\n* **Review Recommendations**\n\nSecondary:\n\n* View details\n* Compare risk\n* Open report\n* Reset view\n* Learn more\n\nAvoid vague CTAs such as:\n\n* Click here\n* Explore now\n* Get started\n\nunless the context makes their meaning clear.\n\n---\n\n# 18. ACCESSIBILITY \342\200\224 WCAG 2.1 AA\n\nImplement accessibility throughout the application.\n\nRequirements:\n\n* Semantic HTML\n* Keyboard navigation\n* Visible focus indicators\n* Proper heading hierarchy\n* Accessible labels\n* Accessible form controls\n* Sufficient color contrast\n* Screen-reader-friendly status messages\n* No color-only information\n* Skip navigation link\n* Accessible modal dialogs\n* Accessible tooltips\n* Reduced-motion support\n* Touch targets approximately 44\303\22744 CSS pixels where practical\n\n### 3D accessibility\n\nThe 3D viewer must have:\n\n* A descriptive accessible label\n* Keyboard controls\n* Text alternative / 2D infrastructure map\n* Asset information available outside the canvas\n* Accessible selected-asset details\n* Non-visual equivalent of risk information\n\nDo not make the 3D canvas the only way to access important information.\n\n---\n\n# 19. RESPONSIVE DESIGN\n\nSupport:\n\n* Desktop\n* Laptop\n* Tablet\n* Mobile\n\n### Desktop\n\nLarge 3D hero and multi-column layouts.\n\n### Tablet\n\nBalanced 3D viewer and content sections.\n\n### Mobile\n\n* 3D viewer becomes a prominent but appropriately sized card\n* Controls remain touch-friendly\n* Content stacks vertically\n* Navigation becomes compact\n* Tables become scrollable or card-based\n* No horizontal overflow\n* Avoid excessive model detail that harms performance\n\nTest at common viewport sizes.\n\n---\n\n# 20. FRONTEND ARCHITECTURE\n\nUse a maintainable component structure.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nsrc/\n  components/\n    layout/\n    navigation/\n    ui/\n    dashboard/\n    risk/\n    infrastructure/\n    three/\n  pages/\n  hooks/\n  services/\n  stores/\n  types/\n  utils/\n  styles/\n```\n\nCreate reusable components:\n\n* `AppShell`\n* `Sidebar`\n* `TopNavigation`\n* `RiskScoreCard`\n* `AssetRiskCard`\n* `InfrastructureViewer`\n* `AssetDetailPanel`\n* `RiskLegend`\n* `ViewerControls`\n* `LoadingState`\n* `ErrorState`\n* `WebGLFallback`\n* `AccessibleDataTable`\n\nDo not duplicate layout or styling logic unnecessarily.\n\n---\n\n# 21. STATE MANAGEMENT\n\nUse a suitable state-management approach.\n\nSeparate:\n\n* UI state\n* Viewer state\n* Application data\n* Loading state\n* Error state\n* User preferences\n\nPersist only appropriate preferences, such as:\n\n* Theme\n* Viewer quality\n* Reduced-motion preference\n* Sidebar state\n\nDo not store secrets in local storage.\n\n---\n\n# 22. BACKEND INTEGRATION\n\nIf backend functionality is included, use a clean API layer.\n\nSupport endpoints such as:\n\n```text\nGET /api/dashboard/summary\nGET /api/assets\nGET /api/assets/{id}\nGET /api/assets/{id}/risk\nGET /api/detections\nGET /api/risk/trends\nGET /api/recommendations\nGET /api/infrastructure/graph\n```\n\nUse typed API responses.\n\nThe 3D viewer should consume infrastructure data from the API rather than hard-coding all asset information into the component.\n\nFor initial development, provide realistic synthetic seed data.\n\nClearly label demo data.\n\n---\n\n# 23. REAL-TIME UPDATES\n\nSupport real-time or near-real-time updates using:\n\n* WebSockets\n* Server-Sent Events\n* Polling fallback\n\nWhen risk changes:\n\n* Update risk cards\n* Update affected asset state\n* Update 3D indicator\n* Update risk trend\n* Show a subtle notification\n* Avoid disruptive page reloads\n\n---\n\n# 24. ERROR HANDLING\n\nCreate polished states for:\n\n* 3D model loading\n* 3D model failure\n* WebGL unavailable\n* API unavailable\n* Empty data\n* Network timeout\n* Unauthorized access\n* Invalid asset\n* Failed report generation\n\nEvery error state must provide a useful next action.\n\n---\n\n# 25. SECURITY REQUIREMENTS\n\nImplement secure frontend practices:\n\n* Validate API responses\n* Avoid unsafe HTML injection\n* Sanitize user-generated content\n* Use secure authentication integration\n* Avoid exposing secrets\n* Configure strict CORS on the backend\n* Use HTTPS in production\n* Avoid embedding untrusted scripts\n* Apply Content Security Policy where practical\n* Do not execute arbitrary code from user input\n\nIf file uploads or external 3D assets are supported:\n\n* Validate file type\n* Validate file size\n* Restrict upload sources\n* Sanitize filenames\n* Do not execute uploaded files\n* Load only trusted model resources\n\n---\n\n# 26. TESTING\n\nImplement:\n\n### Unit tests\n\n* UI components\n* Risk formatting\n* Data transformation\n* Viewer state\n* Accessibility helpers\n\n### Integration tests\n\n* API integration\n* Asset selection\n* Risk updates\n* Navigation\n* Error states\n\n### End-to-end tests\n\nUse Playwright or equivalent.\n\nTest:\n\n```text\nOpen dashboard\n\342\206\222 Navigate to infrastructure\n\342\206\222 Load 3D viewer\n\342\206\222 Select asset\n\342\206\222 Open risk details\n\342\206\222 Navigate to recommendations\n```\n\n### Accessibility testing\n\nUse:\n\n* axe-core\n* Keyboard navigation testing\n* Contrast testing\n\n### Performance testing\n\nMeasure:\n\n* Initial load\n* 3D asset load time\n* Interaction responsiveness\n* Mobile performance\n* WebGL fallback behavior\n\n---\n\n# 27. CODE QUALITY\n\nUse:\n\n* TypeScript strict mode\n* ESLint\n* Prettier\n* Clear naming\n* Small reusable components\n* No unnecessary `any`\n* No dead code\n* No duplicated constants\n* No hard-coded secrets\n* No fake functionality presented as complete\n\nDocument complex 3D rendering decisions.\n\n---\n\n# 28. CI/CD\n\nCreate a CI pipeline that runs:\n\n```text\nInstall dependencies\n      \342\206\223\nLint\n      \342\206\223\nType check\n      \342\206\223\nUnit tests\n      \342\206\223\nBuild\n      \342\206\223\nAccessibility checks\n      \342\206\223\nEnd-to-end tests\n      \342\206\223\nProduction artifact\n```\n\nAdd dependency and security scanning.\n\n---\n\n# 29. DEPLOYMENT\n\nProvide deployment configuration for:\n\n* Local development\n* Vercel / Netlify / equivalent frontend hosting\n* Cloud deployment\n* Optional Docker deployment\n\nEnsure:\n\n* Environment variables are documented\n* Production build works\n* Asset paths work in production\n* 3D model loading works from the deployed origin\n* CORS is configured correctly\n* Error handling works without exposing stack traces\n\n---\n\n# 30. DOCUMENTATION\n\nCreate:\n\n```text\nREADME.md\nARCHITECTURE.md\n3D_IMPLEMENTATION.md\nDESIGN_SYSTEM.md\nACCESSIBILITY.md\nPERFORMANCE.md\nDEPLOYMENT.md\nTESTING.md\nCONTRIBUTING.md\n```\n\nExplain:\n\n* How the 3D viewer works\n* How assets are loaded\n* How the model is optimized\n* How risk data connects to the model\n* How the fallback works\n* How to add new assets\n* How to run the project\n* How to deploy it\n\n---\n\n# 31. IMPLEMENTATION STRATEGY\n\nBuild in the following order:\n\n## Phase 1 \342\200\224 Foundation\n\n* Initialize project\n* Configure TypeScript\n* Set up routing\n* Create design system\n* Build application shell\n\n## Phase 2 \342\200\224 Core UI\n\n* Dashboard\n* Navigation\n* Risk cards\n* Asset panels\n* Responsive layout\n\n## Phase 3 \342\200\224 3D Viewer\n\n* Three.js / React Three Fiber setup\n* Camera\n* Lighting\n* Controls\n* Model loading\n* Responsive canvas\n\n## Phase 4 \342\200\224 3D Intelligence\n\n* Asset mapping\n* Risk indicators\n* Selection\n* Camera focus\n* Data-flow animation\n* Risk overlays\n\n## Phase 5 \342\200\224 Backend Integration\n\n* API layer\n* Synthetic data\n* Asset data\n* Risk updates\n* Real-time state\n\n## Phase 6 \342\200\224 Quality\n\n* Accessibility\n* Performance optimization\n* Error states\n* Testing\n* Deployment\n\n---\n\n# 32. FINAL ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\n\nThe application must satisfy all of the following:\n\n* [ ] Premium, cohesive UI/UX\n* [ ] Responsive across desktop, tablet, and mobile\n* [ ] Interactive 3D centerpiece\n* [ ] Realistic PBR materials\n* [ ] Appropriate lighting\n* [ ] Rotate, zoom, and pan controls\n* [ ] Touch gesture support\n* [ ] Progressive 3D loading\n* [ ] Low-end device optimization\n* [ ] WebGL fallback\n* [ ] Accessible 2D alternative\n* [ ] WCAG 2.1 AA-oriented implementation\n* [ ] Risk-linked 3D assets\n* [ ] Functional navigation\n* [ ] Realistic application data\n* [ ] Error and loading states\n* [ ] Secure API integration\n* [ ] Tests\n* [ ] CI/CD\n* [ ] Documentation\n* [ ] Production build\n\n---\n\n# 33. IMPORTANT EXECUTION RULES\n\n1. Do not generate a static mockup only.\n2. Do not use a placeholder cube as the final 3D centerpiece.\n3. Do not make the 3D viewer purely decorative.\n4. Do not sacrifice usability for visual effects.\n5. Do not use excessive animations.\n6. Do not hard-code sensitive data.\n7. Do not fabricate real cybersecurity incidents.\n8. Use synthetic data for demonstrations.\n9. Clearly distinguish simulated data from live integrations.\n10. Ensure important information remains accessible without WebGL.\n11. Implement real loading, error, and fallback states.\n12. Keep the code modular and maintainable.\n13. Test the application before declaring it complete.\n14. Provide setup and deployment instructions.\n15. If a requested feature cannot be fully implemented, explain the limitation and provide the best working alternative rather than creating a fake feature.\n\n---\n\n# 34. FINAL DELIVERABLE\n\nGenerate the complete application with:\n\n* Source code\n* Functional frontend\n* Interactive 3D viewer\n* Responsive design\n* Synthetic cybersecurity data\n* Risk visualization\n* API integration structure\n* Tests\n* CI/CD configuration\n* Documentation\n* Deployment instructions\n\nAt the end, provide:\n\n1. Project structure\n2. Installation commands\n3. Environment variables\n4. Development commands\n5. Production build commands\n6. Feature completion status\n7. Known limitations\n8. Recommended next improvements\n\nBuild the application as a **polished, production-oriented 3D cybersecurity intelligence experience**, with the 3D model serving as the central interface for understanding infrastructure and cyber risk.\n"
  }
}
